// THE APE ATOLL DUNGEON COULD NOT HURT ANYBODY.
//
// Reported from play as "no rocks falling when in cave on your way to zooknock". What the cave is
// missing is worse than rocks: the 377 cache places NINETY-ONE traps across its four mapsquares and
// not one of them had a script behind it. Seventy-two mm_floorspikes animating away under the
// player's feet, fifteen mm_speartrap, two mm_double_springtrap with their own searchable triggers -
// all drawn, all doing nothing. The whole walk to Zooknock cost not one hitpoint.
//
// Everything used below is the cache's own, which is why this is a build and not an invention:
//
//   mm_floorspikes                "Floor spikes / Human sieve creation", animated, one tile, 72 of them
//   mm_speartrap                  "Slight indentations / Looks suspicious", width 2, 15 of them
//   mm_double_springtrap          "Stalagmites", width 3, with mm_double_springtrap_trigger on the
//                                 middle tile of each - 2 of them
//   mm_crossing_plank             "Plank", for laying over the spring trap
//   speartrap_release             the spear trap firing
//   double_springtrap_release     the spring trap firing, and double_springtrap_reset closing again
//
// And it is written against the one the engine already has: [timer,upass_trap] in
// quest_upass/scripts/upass_obstacles.rs2. These are the SAME traps - mm_speartrap is
// upass_speartrap recoloured, mm_double_springtrap_trigger is upass_logtrap_trigger_0 recoloured -
// so the damage formulas, the "Ouch!", the reset delay and the plank crossing are that file's,
// not numbers made up here. Old School gives one figure of its own, that the floor spikes "hit 4 if
// stood on" and "will continuously hit you until you move or die", and that is ^mm_floorspike_damage.
//
// A greegree does NOT help. Old School is explicit that in monkey form "damage will still be taken
// from traps and falling rocks", so nothing here asks ~mm_is_monkey.
//
// THE TIMER RUNS EVERY TICK, AND ONLY WHERE A TRAP IS. A walking player covers one tile a tick, so
// a trap underfoot can never be stepped over; a running player covers two, which is how Old School's
// "run trick" skips them. The 45 zone declarations below are every 8x8 zone that holds a trap tile,
// generated from the four mapsquares rather than typed - the same reason upass_grid_traps is on
// [zone] and not [mapzone]: "want to avoid running 1t timers too often".

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The 45 zones that hold a trap
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[zone,0_42_142_0_16] settimer(mm_dungeon_trap, 1);
[zone,0_42_142_0_24] settimer(mm_dungeon_trap, 1);
[zone,0_42_142_8_16] settimer(mm_dungeon_trap, 1);
[zone,0_42_142_8_24] settimer(mm_dungeon_trap, 1);
